1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
Welcome to Rashi in Brief, where each week

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,368
we explore the Torah portion through the brilliant

3
00:00:05,388 --> 00:00:08,648
and penetrating eyes of Rashi, the great medieval

4
00:00:08,708 --> 00:00:09,448
commentator.

5
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,368
This week's portion is Ki Teitzei, found in

6
00:00:13,421 --> 00:00:17,128
Deuteronomy chapters twenty-one through twenty-five, and it is

7
00:00:17,197 --> 00:00:20,728
packed with laws, moral teachings, and some truly

8
00:00:20,788 --> 00:00:25,860
fascinating details that Rashi brings to life.Ki Teitzei

9
00:00:25,913 --> 00:00:29,464
contains more individual commandments than any other Torah

10
00:00:29,524 --> 00:00:32,104
portion, over seventy in total.

11
00:00:32,344 --> 00:00:36,104
It covers an extraordinary range of topics: the

12
00:00:36,152 --> 00:00:38,744
treatment of a captive woman in wartime, the

13
00:00:38,801 --> 00:00:43,464
rebellious son, returning lost property, building safety railings

14
00:00:43,491 --> 00:00:47,544
on rooftops, honest weights and measures, marriage and

15
00:00:47,614 --> 00:00:51,544
divorce, treatment of workers, care for the vulnerable,

16
00:00:51,644 --> 00:00:52,825
and much more.

17
00:00:52,974 --> 00:00:56,424
Because the portion moves so rapidly from one

18
00:00:56,504 --> 00:00:59,624
law to the next, Rashi is especially focused

19
00:00:59,688 --> 00:01:02,904
here on the connections between seemingly unrelated laws

20
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:04,744
placed side by side.

21
00:01:05,037 --> 00:01:08,104
He asks again and again: why does the

22
00:01:08,144 --> 00:01:10,505
Torah put this law right next to that

23
00:01:10,585 --> 00:01:10,905
one?

24
00:01:11,198 --> 00:01:15,305
He also digs into unusual word choices, apparent

25
00:01:15,364 --> 00:01:18,345
contradictions, and the gap between what the text

26
00:01:18,393 --> 00:01:21,385
literally says and what the tradition understands it

27
00:01:21,438 --> 00:01:21,945
to mean.

28
00:01:22,169 --> 00:01:26,505
This is Rashi at his most precise, showing

29
00:01:26,558 --> 00:01:28,265
us that even in a long list of

30
00:01:28,361 --> 00:01:34,433
laws, every detail and every sequence carries meaning.Let's

31
00:01:34,486 --> 00:01:36,833
begin with one of the most striking passages

32
00:01:36,886 --> 00:01:39,639
in the entire Torah: the law of the

33
00:01:39,695 --> 00:01:40,999
beautiful captive.

34
00:01:41,239 --> 00:01:43,799
The Torah says that when a soldier goes

35
00:01:43,852 --> 00:01:45,959
to war and sees among the captives a

36
00:01:46,015 --> 00:01:48,439
beautiful woman, he may take her as a

37
00:01:48,519 --> 00:01:51,719
wife, but only after a specific process.

38
00:01:51,899 --> 00:01:54,839
She must let her nails grow long, remove

39
00:01:54,859 --> 00:01:57,399
her fine clothing, sit in his house and

40
00:01:57,479 --> 00:01:59,799
weep for her parents for a full month.

41
00:02:00,172 --> 00:02:03,746
Rashi asks the big question: why would the

42
00:02:03,772 --> 00:02:05,879
Torah permit this at all?

43
00:02:06,119 --> 00:02:08,919
His answer is startlingly honest.

44
00:02:09,059 --> 00:02:12,280
The Torah is speaking, he says, only in

45
00:02:12,328 --> 00:02:15,960
view of the evil inclination, the yetzer hara.

46
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,363
God knows that if the Torah forbade this

47
00:02:18,443 --> 00:02:21,163
entirely, the soldier would take her anyway in

48
00:02:21,227 --> 00:02:22,603
violation of the law.

49
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:25,326
So the Torah makes a concession to human

50
00:02:25,388 --> 00:02:28,526
weakness, but then immediately builds in a process

51
00:02:28,579 --> 00:02:30,686
designed to cool the soldier's passion.

52
00:02:30,886 --> 00:02:33,326
She lets her nails grow so she becomes

53
00:02:33,374 --> 00:02:34,366
less attractive.

54
00:02:34,526 --> 00:02:38,526
She removes her fine garments because, Rashi explains,

55
00:02:38,606 --> 00:02:41,566
the women of enemy nations would deliberately adorn

56
00:02:41,617 --> 00:02:44,366
themselves during wartime to tempt soldiers.

57
00:02:44,586 --> 00:02:48,286
She sits in his house, crying and disheveled,

58
00:02:48,339 --> 00:02:50,206
so that the novelty of her beauty wears

59
00:02:50,306 --> 00:02:50,686
off.

60
00:02:50,926 --> 00:02:54,526
And then Rashi makes a brilliant observation about

61
00:02:54,546 --> 00:02:56,286
the sequence of laws that follow.

62
00:02:56,446 --> 00:02:59,086
After the captive woman comes the law of

63
00:02:59,106 --> 00:03:02,446
the hated wife, and after that, the rebellious

64
00:03:02,546 --> 00:03:03,006
son.

65
00:03:03,230 --> 00:03:08,046
This juxtaposition, Rashi says, is deliberate: if a

66
00:03:08,126 --> 00:03:11,326
man marries through unbridled desire, he will come

67
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:14,206
to hate his wife, and ultimately produce a

68
00:03:14,286 --> 00:03:15,406
wayward child.

69
00:03:15,686 --> 00:03:20,494
One bad decision cascades into another.Speaking of that

70
00:03:20,540 --> 00:03:24,574
rebellious son, the ben sorer u'moreh, Rashi offers

71
00:03:24,614 --> 00:03:27,854
a comment that has challenged readers for centuries.

72
00:03:27,994 --> 00:03:30,817
The Torah prescribes the death penalty for a

73
00:03:30,897 --> 00:03:32,977
son who is a glutton and a drunkard

74
00:03:33,097 --> 00:03:35,697
and refuses to obey his parents.

75
00:03:35,964 --> 00:03:39,857
Rashi explains that the Hebrew word sorer means

76
00:03:39,917 --> 00:03:42,977
one who deviates from the proper path, and

77
00:03:43,057 --> 00:03:46,737
moreh means one who is disobedient, rebellious.

78
00:03:46,957 --> 00:03:49,857
But here is the remarkable part.

79
00:03:50,017 --> 00:03:52,497
Rashi tells us that this son is not

80
00:03:52,586 --> 00:03:55,057
executed for what he has done so far.

81
00:03:55,183 --> 00:03:59,381
Rather, the Torah has fathomed his ultimate disposition.

82
00:03:59,557 --> 00:04:03,061
Left unchecked, he will exhaust his father's wealth,

83
00:04:03,189 --> 00:04:06,261
turn to robbery on the crossroads, and eventually

84
00:04:06,318 --> 00:04:08,421
commit crimes deserving death.

85
00:04:08,781 --> 00:04:13,381
The Torah says, Rashi explains, let him die

86
00:04:13,434 --> 00:04:16,581
while still innocent of those greater crimes, rather

87
00:04:16,613 --> 00:04:18,101
than die guilty of them.

88
00:04:18,448 --> 00:04:21,621
It is a stunning and deeply uncomfortable piece

89
00:04:21,648 --> 00:04:24,821
of reasoning, one that the Talmud itself struggles

90
00:04:24,869 --> 00:04:28,261
with, and it reveals how seriously Rashi takes

91
00:04:28,281 --> 00:04:30,583
the idea that the Torah sees the full

92
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:33,303
trajectory of a life, not just a single

93
00:04:33,383 --> 00:04:37,453
moment.Now let's turn to something gentler but equally

94
00:04:37,533 --> 00:04:41,133
profound: the commandment to send away the mother

95
00:04:41,229 --> 00:04:41,773
bird.

96
00:04:42,013 --> 00:04:44,573
The Torah says that if you come upon

97
00:04:44,653 --> 00:04:46,573
a bird's nest with the mother sitting on

98
00:04:46,593 --> 00:04:48,893
the eggs or young, you must send the

99
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:51,293
mother away before taking the young.

100
00:04:51,513 --> 00:04:55,053
The Torah promises a reward for this, saying

101
00:04:55,069 --> 00:04:56,813
that it may be well with you and

102
00:04:56,833 --> 00:04:58,413
you may prolong your days.

103
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:01,773
Rashi seizes on this and makes an argument

104
00:05:01,805 --> 00:05:03,293
from the lesser to the greater.

105
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,733
If for such an easy commandment, one that

106
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:10,336
costs you nothing financially, the Torah promises long

107
00:05:10,432 --> 00:05:13,536
life and well-being, how much greater must be

108
00:05:13,556 --> 00:05:16,176
the reward for commandments that are difficult to

109
00:05:16,256 --> 00:05:16,896
fulfill?

110
00:05:17,176 --> 00:05:21,856
And then Rashi does something characteristic: he connects

111
00:05:21,904 --> 00:05:23,696
this law to the ones that follow.

112
00:05:24,016 --> 00:05:26,736
If you fulfill the mitzvah of sending the

113
00:05:26,782 --> 00:05:29,936
mother bird, he says, you will merit building

114
00:05:30,016 --> 00:05:32,496
a new house, and then you'll need to

115
00:05:32,546 --> 00:05:35,776
fulfill the commandment of building a safety railing,

116
00:05:35,836 --> 00:05:38,256
and then you'll come to own a vineyard,

117
00:05:38,276 --> 00:05:40,576
and fields, and fine garments.

118
00:05:40,836 --> 00:05:44,256
One good deed leads to another, just as

119
00:05:44,316 --> 00:05:48,807
earlier, one sin led to another.Another comment worth

120
00:05:48,879 --> 00:05:52,167
exploring involves the laws of returning lost property.

121
00:05:52,367 --> 00:05:54,967
The Torah says you must not see your

122
00:05:55,007 --> 00:05:57,847
brother's ox or sheep going astray and hide

123
00:05:57,900 --> 00:05:58,887
yourself from them.

124
00:05:59,180 --> 00:06:02,727
Rashi notices the unusual grammar of the Hebrew

125
00:06:02,796 --> 00:06:06,967
phrase v'hit'alamta, and you shall hide yourself.

126
00:06:07,127 --> 00:06:10,407
He explains that this means closing one's eyes,

127
00:06:10,472 --> 00:06:12,007
pretending not to see.

128
00:06:12,207 --> 00:06:15,687
But the rabbis, Rashi adds, found a deeper

129
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:19,127
teaching in the precise wording: there are indeed

130
00:06:19,220 --> 00:06:21,607
times when you may hide yourself, when you

131
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:24,487
are permitted not to return the lost object,

132
00:06:24,607 --> 00:06:27,527
for instance if retrieving it would compromise your

133
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:29,607
own dignity in a way the law does

134
00:06:29,687 --> 00:06:33,287
not require, or if other legal exemptions apply.

135
00:06:33,479 --> 00:06:37,287
Here we see Rashi's method beautifully: he gives

136
00:06:37,367 --> 00:06:40,087
us the plain meaning first, then opens a

137
00:06:40,151 --> 00:06:44,951
door to the broader legal discussion.Finally, consider Rashi's

138
00:06:45,001 --> 00:06:47,513
comment on why the Torah treats Ammon and

139
00:06:47,593 --> 00:06:50,713
Moab more harshly than Egypt and Edom.

140
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:54,713
Egypt enslaved Israel and drowned their children, and

141
00:06:54,820 --> 00:06:57,193
Edom came out against them with the sword.

142
00:06:57,333 --> 00:07:00,633
Yet the Torah says not to utterly abhor

143
00:07:00,686 --> 00:07:02,873
an Egyptian or an Edomite.

144
00:07:02,961 --> 00:07:06,873
Meanwhile, the Ammonites and Moabites, who hired Balaam

145
00:07:06,926 --> 00:07:09,833
to curse Israel and tempted them into sin,

146
00:07:09,893 --> 00:07:13,913
are permanently excluded from marrying into the community.

147
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:17,353
Rashi explains the reasoning with a powerful moral

148
00:07:17,425 --> 00:07:21,033
principle: one who causes another person to sin

149
00:07:21,097 --> 00:07:23,353
does him greater harm than one who kills

150
00:07:23,393 --> 00:07:26,313
him, because killing removes a person only from

151
00:07:26,377 --> 00:07:29,433
this world, while leading someone to sin removes

152
00:07:29,493 --> 00:07:31,993
him from both this world and the world

153
00:07:32,020 --> 00:07:32,793
to come.

154
00:07:32,905 --> 00:07:36,393
This is a remarkable ethical teaching embedded in

155
00:07:36,425 --> 00:07:39,113
what might look like a simple legal distinction

156
00:07:39,163 --> 00:07:44,962
between nations.Stepping back from these individual comments, several

157
00:07:45,031 --> 00:07:48,962
themes emerge across Rashi's commentary on Ki Teitzei.

158
00:07:49,149 --> 00:07:52,802
First, there is the idea of moral causation,

159
00:07:52,834 --> 00:07:55,442
that actions create chains of consequences.

160
00:07:55,662 --> 00:07:58,642
One sin leads to another sin, and one

161
00:07:58,722 --> 00:08:00,722
mitzvah leads to another mitzvah.

162
00:08:01,015 --> 00:08:04,322
Rashi sees this principle woven into the very

163
00:08:04,378 --> 00:08:06,802
structure of the Torah's text, in the way

164
00:08:06,882 --> 00:08:08,642
laws are placed beside each other.

165
00:08:08,836 --> 00:08:13,082
Second, Rashi consistently shows us a Torah that

166
00:08:13,105 --> 00:08:17,208
is deeply realistic about human nature, a Torah

167
00:08:17,224 --> 00:08:19,608
that makes concessions to weakness while building in

168
00:08:19,681 --> 00:08:23,048
safeguards, that judges people not only by their

169
00:08:23,098 --> 00:08:26,488
present actions but by the trajectories those actions

170
00:08:26,568 --> 00:08:27,528
set in motion.

171
00:08:27,755 --> 00:08:31,928
Third, Rashi reveals that even the most technical

172
00:08:31,995 --> 00:08:35,288
legal details carry profound moral weight.

173
00:08:35,728 --> 00:08:38,728
A law about returning lost animals teaches us

174
00:08:38,768 --> 00:08:41,128
about responsibility and integrity.

175
00:08:41,488 --> 00:08:44,968
A law about which nations may intermarry teaches

176
00:08:45,048 --> 00:08:48,088
us that spiritual harm is worse than physical

177
00:08:48,168 --> 00:08:48,568
harm.

178
00:08:48,828 --> 00:08:51,848
For Rashi, there is no such thing as

179
00:08:51,928 --> 00:08:56,906
a merely procedural commandment.Thank you so much for

180
00:08:56,956 --> 00:08:58,986
joining me on Rashi in Brief.

181
00:08:59,226 --> 00:09:02,026
Wishing you a meaningful week of learning, and

182
00:09:02,066 --> 00:09:03,306
I'll see you next time.

